modern_restatement_en:
Describe the apparent phases of the Earth as seen from the Moon, and the eclipse phenomena involving the Earth seen from the Moon. The expected points are:
(i) the Earth's phase is complementary to the lunar phase seen from Earth, so 'full Earth' occurs at new Moon;
(ii) because of synchronous rotation, the Earth stays nearly fixed in the lunar sky, oscillating only by libration ($\approx\pm 8^\circ$), and is never visible from most of the far side;
(iii) a terrestrial lunar eclipse is, seen from the Moon, an eclipse of the Sun by the Earth, with a reddish refraction ring;
(iv) a terrestrial solar eclipse appears from the Moon as the small dark penumbral/umbral spot of the Moon's shadow crossing the Earth's disc.
